libcutils: ashmem: recognise a memfd by its seals when /proc/self/fd is not readable

Chromium renderers (seccomp: file system calls fail with EPERM) cannot
readlink /proc/self/fd/N, so is_memfd_fd() failed, the ashmem fallback
found no /dev/ashmem (mainline kernel) and every shared memory region
was invalid there: web pages rendered blank in Cromite and the
LineageOS browser (LineageOS 24 on the ZTE Axon 7, sys.use_memfd=true).

--- a/libcutils/ashmem-dev.cpp	2026-11-15 17:43:50.689203388 +0100
+++ b/libcutils/ashmem-dev.cpp	2026-11-15 17:43:50.703203411 +0100
@@ -198,11 +198,17 @@
     std::string fd_path = android::base::StringPrintf("/proc/self/fd/%d", fd);
     std::string result;
     if (!android::base::Readlink(fd_path, &result)) {
-        ALOGE("readlink(%s) failed: %m", fd_path.c_str());
-        return false;
-    }
-
-    if (!result.starts_with("/memfd:")) {
+        // Sandboxed processes cannot always read /proc/self/fd: Chromium's
+        // renderer seccomp policy answers file system calls with EPERM.
+        // Without /dev/ashmem (mainline kernels) every region there would
+        // then be invalid and web content stays blank. Fall back to the
+        // seals below: only a memfd can carry F_SEAL_GROW|F_SEAL_SHRINK
+        // (other shmem files start with F_SEAL_SEAL and cannot get seals).
+        if (errno != EPERM && errno != EACCES) {
+            ALOGE("readlink(%s) failed: %m", fd_path.c_str());
+            return false;
+        }
+    } else if (!result.starts_with("/memfd:")) {
         return false;
     }
 
